  <!--
    1.12.124 -> 1.12.125, UNIT 271. A PATCH, AND IT REPAIRS THE FIRST DEFECT THIS PROJECT HAS PUT ON
    A LIVE ANTENNA. Hamlet transmitted twice on 14.074000 on 2026-09-07 and the machinery was clean -
    keyed 0.5 s in, 606720 samples, Sent, OrdinaryUnkey. WHAT IT SAID WAS NOT. The operator's grid in
    Settings is six characters, FN00DJ, and a standard FT8 message carries four. MEASURED, task 1,
    through Ft8Composer and back through Ft8Sharp's own Ft8SlotDecoder at 12000 and packed again at
    the 48000 the endpoint actually ran at:

      "VP2MAA KC3QIS FN00DJ" -> NonstandardCallsign, the bits say "<VP2MAA KC3QIS> FN00DJ",
                                callsign hashed, DECODER RETURNS NOTHING.
      "CQ KC3QIS FN00DJ"     -> Standard, the bits say "<CQ KC3QIS> <FN00DJ>",
                                callsign hashed, DECODER RETURNS NOTHING.
      "VP2MAA KC3QIS FN00"   -> Standard, reads back as itself.
      "CQ KC3QIS FN00"       -> Standard, reads back as itself.

    THE SIX-CHARACTER GRID WAS NOT TRUNCATED AND WAS NOT REJECTED - it was encoded as something
    else. The standard packing of "VP2MAA" / "KC3QIS" / "FN00DJ" succeeds and reads back
    "VP2MAA KC3QIS FN00", so Ft8Composer's round-trip guard correctly refused it as a truncation;
    the words then fell through to the pass that allows a callsign on the wire as a hash, where
    "VP2MAA KC3QIS" hashed as ONE callsign field and the bracket-stripping comparison accepted it.
    A transmission asserting something nobody receives is 0.0 pointed the other way, and it went out
    on a live antenna twice.

    THE REPAIR IS AT THE ONE PLACE A GRID ENTERS A TRANSMITTED MESSAGE - Ft8SendOptions - and the
    six characters stay in Settings untouched, because distance and bearing use them. The round trip
    is now the test: compose, encode, decode with Ft8Sharp, get the same text back, over a CQ, a grid
    reply, a report, an R- report, RRR, RR73 and 73.

    ALSO IN: the CQ button's independence from the decoded table is pinned by test rather than by
    reading; the contact column speaks only where the message is addressed to the operator, so a CQ
    and two other stations working each other say nothing there; and the stop control carries a label
    and a resting state, with the abort behind it untouched. SHACK_FACTS.md gains the level the
    operator measured at his own radio, which no machine in this repository could know.
  -->
  <!--
    1.12.125 -> 1.12.126, UNIT 272. A PATCH, AND IT CLOSES THE CLASS UNIT 271 REPAIRED ONE MEMBER OF.
    Unit 271 fixed the input that made both live transmissions unreadable - the six-character grid -
    and left the hole that let it happen open. Ft8Composer computes, per transmission, whether the
    message travels with a callsign as a 22-bit hash, and hands it back as
    Ft8Transmission.CarriesHashedCallsign. NOTHING IN src/ READ IT: grep -rn CarriesHashedCallsign src/
    returned Ft8Composer.cs and nothing else, so the application composed, saw Composed: true, armed
    and keyed - which is exactly what it did twice on 14.074.

    MEASURED, task 1, through the application's own route at 48000 Hz and read back through
    Ft8Resample and Ft8Sharp's own Ft8SlotDecoder the way Ft8Reception reads every slot:

      15 OF 43 MESSAGES THE MENU CAN OFFER WOULD BE KEYED TODAY AND READ BACK AS NOTHING.

    It is compound PREFIXES, and it is not portable suffixes - "K1ABC/P KC3QIS FN00" packs standard
    and reads back as itself. "VP2M/K1ABC KC3QIS FN00" goes out as "<VP2M/K1ABC> KC3QIS FN00" and the
    decoder returns nothing, on all five menu shapes; so does "SV9/PA3EXX"; and so does the operator's
    OWN compound form, "K1ABC W4/KC3QIS FN00" -> "K1ABC <W4/KC3QIS> FN00".

    THE REFUSAL IS AT THE SEND PATH, WHERE THE LICENCE GATE ALREADY REFUSES, AND NOT IN THE MENU.
    Nothing is removed, greyed, hidden or reordered; the rule lives in the engine as
    Ft8ReadBack.WouldReachAnybody and the view model states no rule of its own. AND THE TELEMETRY LINE
    NO LONGER DESCRIBES A HASHED TRANSMISSION AS A HEALTHY SIXTEEN-CHARACTER STANDARD MESSAGE:
    ft8_transmission gains carriedHashedCallsign, and messageLength now counts the ENCODED message
    rather than the composed string. HM-DEC-018 holds - a type, a flag and a length, and no words.

    No file under src/Ft8Sharp/ changed.
  -->
  <!--
    1.12.223 -> 1.12.224, UNIT 291 TASK 1, UNDER HM-DEC-150. A PATCH, AND IT ADDS NO BEHAVIOUR AT
    ALL: it records the starting position before the log gains a submode.

      THE WALK, AND THE ONE THING IT FOUND THAT A FIELD ALONE WOULD NOT HAVE.
      TheLogCanSayFt4Tests asserts, against today's code, that an FT4 contact CANNOT be expressed -
      AdifContact has twelve public properties and none of them is a submode, a contact driven
      through Ft8ContactLogEntry.For with MFSK conditions writes <MODE:4>MFSK and the string SUBMODE
      appears nowhere in the record, and ContactModes.Named("FT4").Matches("MFSK", null) is false.
      Three tests, three passing, and every one of them is written to go red the moment task 2 lands.

    No file under src/Ft8Sharp/ changed.
  -->
  <!--
    1.12.224 -> 1.12.225, UNIT 291 TASK 2, UNDER HM-DEC-150. A PATCH, AND THE FIRST TIME THE LOG HAS
    ROOM FOR A MODE THAT NEEDS TWO FIELDS TO NAME IT.

      AdifContact gains SUBMODE - thirteen init properties where there were twelve - written beside
      MODE at AdifLog.Record and read beside it in From. MODE=MFSK on its own is some kind of
      multi-frequency shift keying; the pair MODE=MFSK, SUBMODE=FT4 is FT4, and MODE=FT4 is not
      valid ADIF.

      ABSENT IS ABSENT AND THAT IS THE CRITERION A CARELESS IMPLEMENTATION BREAKS. Field already
      returns without writing on a null or empty value, so a contact with no submode produces a
      record in which the string SUBMODE does not appear at all - not <SUBMODE:0>, which would
      assert that an empty submode was observed. The round trip brings it back null and never "".

      AND NOT A BYTE OF AN FT8 RECORD MOVED. AnFt8RecordIsByteIdenticalToWhatItWasBeforeTheSubmode
      pins the whole 245-character record as a single string comparison rather than a Contains.

      THE TAG NAME IS A READING AND IT IS MARKED AS ONE. adif.org could not be reached from this
      session - the shell refused the fetch - and there is no pinned copy under data/vendor/. So
      SUBMODE and the MFSK/FT4 pairing rest on unit 287s in-tree reading of ADIF 3.1.4, carried at
      ContactModes.Cite. Marked the way FREQ is already marked, per 12.4. Not recalled from memory.

    No file under src/Ft8Sharp/ changed.
  -->
  <!--
    1.12.225 -> 1.12.226, UNIT 291 TASK 3, UNDER HM-DEC-150. A PATCH. AN FT4 CONTACT NOW LOGS AS
    MODE=MFSK, SUBMODE=FT4 - AND THE TWO CANNOT BE SET TO DISAGREE.

      Ft8StationConditions.Mode was a string and is now a ContactMode. That is the whole of it. The
      cheap and wrong shape is two independent strings, which is exactly how a record comes to read
      MODE=FT8, SUBMODE=FT4; with one object there is nothing for the halves to disagree about,
      because both tags are projections of it and no assignment anywhere sets one without the other.
      The compiler now refuses a mode that is not one of the six, rather than letting a typo drop
      the field silently - governing principle 0, let the compiler catch it.

      AND VOICE COMES OUT WITH NO MODE AT ALL, WHICH IS THE RIGHT ANSWER. ContactMode.AdifMode is
      null where the mode names more than one ADIF value. Voice is Hamlets own word for SSB, AM and
      FM; writing the first of the three would name a mode the operator may not have worked, and
      there is nothing later that could tell such a record from a true one. Where the mode goes out,
      the submode goes with it: a SUBMODE with no MODE beside it names nothing.

      THE ONE WRITE PATH IS STILL UNCONDITIONALLY FT8. MainWindowViewModel stopped saying the
      literal "FT8" and now says ContactModes.Named("FT8") - a type change, not a condition. What
      makes that line say anything else is the Digital tabs mode wiring, which is step 4s.

      FT8 IS UNCHANGED, ASSERTED RATHER THAN ASSUMED. TheLogEntryIsWhatWasHeardTests, the FT8
      control, is 8 of 8; an FT8 contact still carries MODE=FT8 with the string SUBMODE nowhere in
      the record.

    No file under src/Ft8Sharp/ changed.
  -->
  <!--
    1.12.226 -> 1.12.227, UNIT 291 TASK 4, UNDER HM-DEC-150. A PATCH, AND IT CHANGES WHAT THE SCREEN
    SAYS TO THE OPERATOR.

      AchievementsViewModel.Row handed Matches a literal null for the submode, because AdifContact
      had no such field. It now reads the records own, so an MODE=MFSK, SUBMODE=FT4 record lights
      the FT4 row. A bare MODE=MFSK still lights nothing, deliberately: some kind of multi-frequency
      shift keying read as FT4 would show him a first he had not made.

      AND THE SCREEN STOPPED SAYING HAMLET CANNOT WRITE THE MODE DOWN. FT4s hover gave two reasons
      and one of them stopped being true in task 2 - a screen asserting a limitation that no longer
      exists is the 0.0 fault unit 287 built this card to avoid. It names the one gap that remains:
      Hamlet can tune him to the FT4 frequencies and cannot work them yet. PSK31s opened with "The
      same two things as FT4" and went stale by reference; it says its own reasons now, and notes
      that its record room arrived as a side effect while its send path did not.

      FT4 UNEARNED IS STILL WAITING ON HAMLET, which is the honest answer: one gap where there were
      two. What the log can hold and what Hamlet can write are now different questions.

      TheAchievementsScreenTests is 10 of 10 - unit 287s eight unchanged, and two added. None of the
      eight went red.

    No file under src/Ft8Sharp/ changed.
  -->
  <!--
    1.12.227 -> 1.12.228, UNIT 291 TASK 6, UNDER HM-DEC-150. A PATCH. THE FIELD GUIDE GAINS THE FT4
    ROW UNIT 290s CENSUS FOUND MISSING - THE NAMED DROP CANDIDATE, TAKEN AND NOT DROPPED.

      ModeGuide.cs:127 had sorted the string FT4 into ModeFamily.Digital all along, so every surface
      that colours a mode already had an answer for FT4 while the guide beside it had no entry.

      NOTHING IN THE ROW SAYS HOW LONG A SLOT IS. The 4.48 against 5.04 transmission figure is with
      Tim and a guide stating either would be answering it. The row says half-length slots, which is
      true on both readings, and the test asserts the words 4.48, 5.04, 7.5, seven and a half and
      second appear in none of its three prose fields.

      THE FREQUENCY IS READ AND NOT RECALLED. 7.047 MHz is the jumpHz of the FT4 sprint row for 40 m
      in data/bands/us-neighborhoods.json, which cites the WSJT-X default frequency table.

      AND CHECKING IT AGAINST THE DATA TURNED UP TWO INHERITED DISAGREEMENTS THIS UNIT DID NOT CAUSE
      AND DOES NOT REPAIR. RTTYs field-guide home is 7.062 MHz where the data says 7.040, and PSK31s
      is 7.065 where the data says 7.070. A frequency change needs a citation and which source is
      right is not a sessions to decide. They are asserted as a known set rather than skipped, so a
      third would go red and so would a fix, and they are named in section 4 of the report.

    No file under src/Ft8Sharp/ changed.
  -->
  <!--
